  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 AM MDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nEarly morning satellite imagery shows the stagnant Pacific \nNorthwest upper-level low finally making eastward progress \nacross Oregon. Banded cloud cover and showers continue to stream\naround the eastern flank of the system, maintaining active \nweather over the Central Mountains early this morning. High- \nresolution convective-allowing models (CAMs) indicate mountain \nshowers will taper off around or shortly after sunrise, yielding\na brief mid-day lull across the forecast area.\n\nThis lull will be short-lived as the parent low advances closer\nto Idaho. Strong dynamic lift and surface heating\u2014pushing \nvalley highs into the upper 70s and lower 80s\u2014will yield \nsufficient instability and kinematics for organized convection. \nThe Storm Prediction Center (SPC) maintains a Marginal Risk \nacross much of eastern Idaho for strong to severe thunderstorm \nwind gusts. Most daytime hours will remain pleasant with \nincreasing clouds, as convective development holds off until \nafter 5:00 or 6:00 PM. Southerly surface winds ahead of the main\nline will increase to 15\u201325 mph with gusts reaching 30\u201340 mph. \nConvective activity will persist through the evening and early \novernight hours before diminishing toward Labor Day morning. \nWhile the overall storm system has trended drier compared to \nearlier model runs, localized heavy downpours remain possible \nbeneath stronger convective cores.\n\nLabor Day Monday will be markedly cooler behind the cold front,\nwith daytime highs capped in the 60s across most valley \nlocations, though a few spots near the Utah border may briefly \ntouch the lower 70s. Showers will wane by Monday afternoon, \nleaving dry but cool conditions for the evening. Clear skies and\ncold advection Monday night into Tuesday morning will result in\nthe coldest temperatures of the week, with high country basins \ndropping into the 30s and lower elevation valleys falling into \nthe 40s. Tuesday will be pleasant, dry, and seasonably cool.\n\nTemperatures moderate rapidly back toward seasonal averages on\nWednesday, with valley highs returning to the upper 70s and\nlower 80s. High pressure and general zonal upper flow will keep\nconditions mostly dry and uneventful through Friday, though\nisolated higher-terrain showers cannot be entirely ruled out as\nshort-range hi-res guidance may better resolve subtle impulses.\nThe next potential for widespread precipitation arrives next\nweekend as global models track an upper-level trough southward\nout of Western Canada. However, considerable ensemble spread\nremains regarding the depth, track, and associated surface\nimpacts of this feature.",
